  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual landscape showing how warming permafrost affects both natural terrain and built infrastructure in a cold mountain-to-coastal region. Across the scene, several hazards are highlighted: slow and rapid mass wasting on steep slopes, thaw of ice-rich permafrost that produces thermokarst, and thermal erosion along a coastal edge where houses are shown near collapsing ground. A road and urban or industrial buildings lie within this thawing terrain, suggesting that settlement and transport networks are exposed to instability.\n\nAn oil refinery and pipelines are depicted over the permafrost, with dark leakage-like shapes near the pipes, implying that ground thaw and deformation can damage energy infrastructure and trigger spills. The inset cross-section explains the subsurface mechanism. It shows a pile-supported building above permafrost, with a shallow active layer at the surface. As temperatures rise, the active layer thickens and deeper thawed zones, labeled taliks, develop beneath and between the piles. This illustrates how permafrost warming reduces ground stability, undermines foundations, and contributes to broader slope failure, erosion, and infrastructure risk throughout the landscape.",
